      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gustavo A. Madero began as Villa de Guadalupe in 1563 before becoming a borough in 1931.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustavo_A._Madero%2C_Mexico_City",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough takes its name from revolutionary leader Gustavo Adolfo Madero, brother of President Francisco I. Alfonso.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.trawellino.com/destinations/mexico/mexico-city_gustavo-adolfo-madero",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The New Basilica of Santa María de Guadalupe, opened in the 1970s, lets visitors view the revered image from every angle.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://mexicocity.cdmx.gob.mx/locations/gustavo-a-madero/?lang=en",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo, ending the Mexican–American War, was signed in this borough in 1848.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustavo_A._Madero%2C_Mexico_City",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every Sunday, the San Felipe de Jesús Tianguis stretches seven kilometers and hosts about 30,000 vendors, making it Latin America’s largest street market.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustavo_A._Madero%2C_Mexico_City",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Gustavo A. Madero houses Mexico City’s Northern Central Bus Station, a hub that links the city to northern and western destinations.",
